WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.620
Where can a trait hide for a whole generation?

2
00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:08.720
Mendel asked that of garden peas.

3
00:00:09.833 --> 00:00:12.453
He started with lines that always bred true.

4
00:00:13.793 --> 00:00:15.713
One line always gave round seeds.

5
00:00:16.933 --> 00:00:18.873
The other always gave wrinkled seeds.

6
00:00:19.917 --> 00:00:21.537
Then he crossed them by hand.

7
00:00:22.737 --> 00:00:27.657
He opened a young flower, removed its stamens, which make pollen, and brushed on

8
00:00:27.657 --> 00:00:28.857
pollen from the other line.

9
00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:33.080
Every hybrid seed was round, like the round parent.

10
00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:35.480
Not halfway between.

11
00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:38.080
Wrinkled seemed to be gone.

12
00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:43.550
He planted those round hybrid seeds and let each plant pollinate itself.

13
00:00:44.870 --> 00:00:48.150
Their pods held round and wrinkled peas, side by side.

14
00:00:49.390 --> 00:00:50.890
Wrinkled was back, unchanged.

15
00:00:52.083 --> 00:00:54.143
He counted thousands of these seeds.

16
00:00:55.543 --> 00:00:57.543
Round beat wrinkled by about 3 to 1.

17
00:00:58.803 --> 00:01:01.203
So where was wrinkled hiding, and why one in four?

18
00:01:02.267 --> 00:01:04.627
Here is the answer in modern terms.

19
00:01:05.887 --> 00:01:09.047
Each pea plant carries two copies of the gene for seed shape.

20
00:01:10.307 --> 00:01:11.927
One copy came from each parent.

21
00:01:12.967 --> 00:01:14.687
Picture the copies as cards.

22
00:01:15.947 --> 00:01:17.847
A round line holds two round cards.

23
00:01:19.127 --> 00:01:21.307
A wrinkled line holds two wrinkled cards.

24
00:01:22.467 --> 00:01:23.927
The hybrid holds one of each.

25
00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:29.450
When the hybrid makes an egg or a pollen grain, it puts in just one card.

26
00:01:30.770 --> 00:01:32.990
Which card is decided like a fair coin toss.

27
00:01:34.133 --> 00:01:38.413
So half its eggs carry a round copy, and half carry a wrinkled copy.

28
00:01:39.613 --> 00:01:41.093
The same is true of its pollen.

29
00:01:42.217 --> 00:01:44.237
Now the hybrid pollinates itself.

30
00:01:45.757 --> 00:01:49.137
Eggs meet pollen at random, so four unions are equally likely.

31
00:01:50.517 --> 00:01:52.277
One box gets two round copies.

32
00:01:53.557 --> 00:01:54.557
Two get one of each.

33
00:01:55.797 --> 00:01:57.337
One gets two wrinkled copies.

34
00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:02.510
Remember, every hybrid seed was round, with one copy of each.

35
00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:05.970
So which of these four boxes will look round?

36
00:02:12.567 --> 00:02:17.347
Three of them. One round copy is enough for a round seed.

37
00:02:18.887 --> 00:02:22.187
Mendel called round the dominant form, the one the hybrid shows.

38
00:02:23.350 --> 00:02:25.570
Wrinkled is the recessive form.

39
00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:30.270
It hides in the hybrid, and shows only with two wrinkled copies.

40
00:02:31.433 --> 00:02:33.693
So the hidden mix is 1 : 2 : 1.

41
00:02:34.913 --> 00:02:37.053
What you see is 3 round to 1 wrinkled.

42
00:02:38.017 --> 00:02:39.357
Back to Mendel's count.

43
00:02:40.677 --> 00:02:43.297
It came close to 3 to 1, but not exactly.

44
00:02:44.897 --> 00:02:47.357
Real counts scatter around what the coin toss predicts.

45
00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:50.000
So here is the idea.

46
00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:53.000
Every pea plant has two copies.

47
00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:57.200
Every egg and pollen grain gets one, by a coin toss.

48
00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:00.700
One round copy is enough to look round.

49
00:03:01.783 --> 00:03:04.783
If that is right, the round seeds are not all the same.

50
00:03:06.043 --> 00:03:07.363
Some hide a wrinkled copy.

51
00:03:08.663 --> 00:03:09.863
How could you find out which?
